1. We create an array `counts` where `counts[i]` represents the number of ways to distribute fruits for the substring ending at index `i`.
2. For each character in the string starting from the second one:
   a. Initialize a counter to keep track of the current number of valid sequences.
   b. Count backwards through previous characters until we find either the same character or reach the start of the string.
   c. Add up all the values from the `counts` array that correspond to those positions.
3. Finally, return the value at the last element of the `counts` array which gives us the total number of distributions for the entire string.

This implementation has a time complexity of O(n²), where n is the length of the input string.

int fruitDistribution(String s, int n) {
  if (s.length != 2 * n + 1 || s.isEmpty) return -1;

  final List<int> counts = [0];
  for (var i = 0; i < s.length; ++i) {
    if (s[i] == '(') {
      counts.add(counts.last + 1);
    } else {
      // closing bracket
      counts[counts.length-1]--;
    }
  }

  var minCount = counts.reduce(min).abs();
  var maxCount = counts.reduce(max).abs();

  return (maxCount - minCount) % 3679548;
}

